EXAMPLE 1.2. Let $\Delta = \{0, 1\}$ and let $\mathcal{D} = \{\{0, 1\}, \{0\}, \{1\}\}$.

In pictures we have:

```mermaid
%%{init: {
  "themeCSS": ".cluster rect { rx: 80px; ry: 80px; }",
  "flowchart": { "htmlLabels": true }
}}%%
flowchart LR
  subgraph outer[" "]
    direction LR
    Z(("<span style='font-size:1.6em;line-height:1'>●</span><span style='font-size:1.15em'>0</span>"))
    O(("<span style='font-size:1.6em;line-height:1'>●</span><span style='font-size:1.15em'>1</span>"))
    Z ~~~ O
  end
  style outer fill:none,stroke:#222,stroke-width:1.5px
```

The intention is that 0 and 1 can be completely specified and that they can be identified with the total elements. As we shall see, there is only one partial element: either we give no information (the neighbourhood $\{0, 1\}$), or we decide between 0 and 1 (by giving $\{0\}$ or $\{1\}$). $\square$

The development is with the [two-token system](1980_TwoToken.md) peer.

<!-- formalization -->

**Theorem (`example_1_2`).** There is a neighbourhood system on $\{0,1\}$ whose master set is the full token set and whose neighbourhoods are exactly $\{\{0,1\},\{0\},\{1\}\}$.

*Proof.* By the two-token neighbourhood system. ∎
